process_cpu_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Cumulative user+system CPU seconds consumed by this process (read from /proc/self/stat). Delta / wall-clock delta = CPU utilisation ratio (1.0 = one full core).
process_max_fds	Gauge	Some(Count)	RLIMIT_NOFILE soft limit (/proc/self/limits). Exported so process_open_fds / process_max_fds is a query, not a hardcoded alert threshold.
process_memory_anon_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Anonymous resident memory (/proc/self/smaps_rollup Anonymous): heap + thread stacks — the pages the allocator and code own. The series to chase in a leak hunt; file-backed RSS is reclaimable, this isn't.
process_memory_file_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Non-anonymous resident memory (RSS minus Anonymous, /proc/self/smaps_rollup): file-backed mappings + shmem — the binary, mapped page cache. Kernel-reclaimable under pressure.
process_memory_rss_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Resident set size (VmRSS, /proc/self/status) of this process — the RAM working set. Climbs under write-stall backpressure and precedes an OOM kill (137) if it reaches the container/cgroup limit.
process_open_fds	Gauge	Some(Count)	Open file descriptors (/proc/self/fd). Sockets, listeners and every RocksDB SST and WAL file. Compare against process_max_fds: at the limit, dials AND accepts fail for a local reason, which looks exactly like an unreachable peer but is not one.
process_threads_count	Gauge	Some(Count)	OS threads in this process (Threads:, /proc/self/status). A climb means a growing tokio blocking pool or leaked spawn_blocking work; each thread's touched stack pages are anonymous RSS.
zslg_compaction_runs_total	Counter	None	Manifest compaction runs by outcome.
zslg_coordinator_fan_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time from a write entering pending to the coordinator picking it up to fan it to a follower (pending enqueue -> fan pickup).
zslg_coordinator_permit_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time the feed loop waited for an in-flight permit (the MAX_INFLIGHT semaphore). THE backpressure indicator: when writes accumulate in pending faster than quorum completes, this grows.
zslg_coordinator_quorum_latency_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time from a write entering pending to quorum being reached (the writer's oneshot fires). What the client's write() actually waits for. Compare to the per-follower replica_write_rpc: a gap means the quorum waits on a path the per-follower round trip misses.
zslg_coordinator_submit_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time a write sat in the coordinator's submit channel before the feed loop picked it up (submit() -> feed recv). Grows when the feed loop is backed up (writes not being absorbed).
zslg_hlc_cutoff_drain_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time set_cutoff waited for in-flight HlcGuards to drain before ACKing. Tail growth = slow write paths holding guards.
zslg_hlc_cutoff_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of this replica's HLC cutoff.
zslg_hlc_cutoff_sets_total	Counter	None	Cutoff set attempts on this replica. rejected_backwards is expected once per leader change; alert on sustained rate.
zslg_hlc_drift_rejections_total	Counter	None	Peer HLC integrations rejected as hlc_drift_too_large, by direction. `behind` (older peer stamp) is usually receive-buffer latency under load; `ahead` points at a genuinely fast peer clock.
zslg_hlc_drift_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Absolute drift between a peer HLC physical-ms and local wall clock observed during integrate(). Values beyond the configured skew limit are rejected; the histogram reveals the normal operating band. Under load this is dominated by receive-buffer latency, not clock skew.
zslg_hlc_guards_in_flight	Gauge	Some(Count)	Outstanding HlcGuards (writes admitted but not yet durable + broadcast). Cutoff drain blocks on these.
zslg_hlc_logical	Gauge	Some(Count)	Logical component of the node's current HLC. Sustained high values = wall clock stalled or heavy same-millisecond traffic.
zslg_hlc_now_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of the node's current HLC.
zslg_hlc_skew_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Per-peer HLC physical-ms skew (|local − peer| / 1000) observed on the replication subscriber path. Creeps toward the configured HLC skew limit (`hlc_max_clock_skew`) as a peer approaches rejection — a leading indicator of the drift-reject/reconnect churn loop. Labeled by peer_id.
zslg_hlc_wall_clock_drift_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Signed difference between the HLC physical part and the wall clock. Approaching max_clock_skew = imminent write rejections.
zslg_jemalloc_active_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.active: bytes in whole pages backing allocations. active - allocated = internal fragmentation. Only emitted with the `jemalloc` feature.
zslg_jemalloc_allocated_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.allocated: bytes the application has live-allocated (the true heap working set). Only emitted when the binary is built with the `jemalloc` feature (the harness default).
zslg_jemalloc_mapped_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.mapped: total virtual address space jemalloc has mapped (not physical). Only emitted with the `jemalloc` feature.
zslg_jemalloc_metadata_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.metadata: allocator bookkeeping overhead (page maps, arena structures). Only emitted with the `jemalloc` feature.
zslg_jemalloc_resident_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.resident: physical memory jemalloc holds (its share of RSS). resident - active = dirty pages awaiting decay; RSS - resident = non-heap memory (thread stacks, binary, mmaps). Only emitted with the `jemalloc` feature.
zslg_jemalloc_retained_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	jemalloc stats.retained: virtual address space retained after unmap (not physical memory; cheap to hold). Only emitted with the `jemalloc` feature.
zslg_leader_intent_batches_total	Counter	None	Raft proposals issued by the leader integrator. Divide leader_intents_total{state="applied"} by this for the coalescing factor.
zslg_leader_intents_total	Counter	None	Raft-state intents resolved by the leader integrator, by outcome (applied/already_satisfied/superseded/failed).
zslg_leader_job_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	How long each leader-job iteration took. The snapshot and compaction jobs are the long ones; they run concurrently, so a long tail here does not imply other jobs are blocked.
zslg_leader_job_last_run_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix-epoch seconds when each leader job last completed an iteration. Grafana shows staleness as time() - this; a job that is wedged stops advancing it. Only meaningful on the leader.
zslg_leader_job_runs_total	Counter	None	Completed iterations per leader job.
zslg_leader_tasks_active	Gauge	None	1 while the leader-only background loop runs on this node. Disagreement with raft_is_leader = task lifecycle bug (start-on-promotion / stop-on-demotion).
zslg_local_store_entry_bytes	Histogram	Some(Bytes)	Encoded size of full-data entries written to the local store. _bucket is the payload-size distribution.
zslg_local_store_flush_bytes	Histogram	Some(Bytes)	Bytes written per local-store flush (one WriteBatch). _sum is byte throughput, _count the number of flushes.
zslg_local_store_flush_entries	Histogram	Some(Count)	Operations folded into one local-store flush. Near 1 under load = batching starvation; near the cap = healthy batching.
zslg_local_store_integrate_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	LocalStore::integrate_batch latency by outcome. _count is the integrate throughput.
zslg_local_store_integrate_queue_depth	Gauge	None	Depth of the node's shared local-store integration channel, which every subscription forwards into. Non-zero means the single integration worker is falling behind the incoming streams. Not labeled by peer: there is one queue per node, not one per subscription (it replaced the per-peer subscription_buffer_depth when the per-subscription queue was removed).
zslg_local_store_integrate_skipped_duplicate_total	Counter	None	Duplicate copies of an already-written data_id dropped before the LSM write. Every learner subscribes to every replica, so each write arrives once per replica; this counts copies 2..n. Expect roughly (replicas - 1)/replicas of arrivals. Distinct from skipped_stale, which is about the stable prefix, not duplication.
zslg_local_store_integrate_skipped_stale_total	Counter	None	Subscription records skipped at integrate because the stable prefix already covers them (HLC <= end_hlc). Catch-up redundancy avoided; near-zero in steady state.
zslg_local_store_last_flush_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix timestamp of the last local-store flush (Grafana: time() minus this for 'time since last flush').
zslg_local_store_ops_total	Counter	None	Local-store write operations by kind (integrate).
zslg_log_index_frontier	Gauge	None	Highest log index per log type. replica-vs-local spread on a learner = replication lag in entries.
zslg_log_subscription_catchup_backlog_entries	Gauge	Some(Count)	Entries a subscriber had to replay at its most recent connect (serving replica side): the replica's frontier minus the subscriber's resume cursor. Rising across successive connects means the deficit is diverging — shedding is no longer just protecting the node, it is preventing catch-up. Flat or falling means shedding is working as intended.
zslg_log_subscription_connection_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Lifetime of one subscription attempt (learner side), from connect to stream end. Healthy = long-lived (minutes to hours). A tight cluster of sub-second lifetimes means connections are being ended by buffer pressure and every reconnect re-pays the replica's catch-up scan.
zslg_log_subscription_consumer_stalled_total	Counter	None	Subscriptions ended because the consumer accepted nothing for the whole stall timeout (30s). NOT backpressure: a slow consumer is waited on and is not counted anywhere. This fires only when an integrate loop has stopped making room entirely, which is a bug — an fsync-fenced batch frees a slot in milliseconds. No data is lost (the subscriber resumes from its durable cursor); the thread and socket are released so a wedged consumer cannot pin them forever. MUST be zero.
zslg_log_subscription_ended_total	Counter	None	Subscription attempts that ended, by reason (learner side). "ended": the stream stopped — a socket drop, a replica restart, or a shed on either side, which are indistinguishable on the wire; correlate log_subscription_shed_total on this node and on the serving replica to tell them apart. "peer_gone": the peer is unroutable or replaced (membership converging). "failed": stream or integrate error. A high "ended" rate on a healthy network means the buffers, not the peers, are ending connections.
zslg_log_subscription_messages_total	Counter	None	Records received via log subscription (learner side). rate() compared with log_writes_total{log_type="replica"} on the upstream = per-subscription catch-up lag.
zslg_log_subscription_reconnects_total	Counter	None	Subscription reconnects (learner side). Sustained rate = flaky link or replica restarts.
zslg_log_subscription_records_retired_entries	Histogram	Some(Count)	Durable progress made by one subscription attempt (learner side): the advance of this peer's persisted subscription cursor between connect and stream end. The progress-per-connection measure. A distribution pinned near the combined receive-buffer capacity means each connection dies exactly when its buffers fill, so a reconnect never retires more than a bufferful and the replica's catch-up scan is re-paid every time. Near zero with a non-zero reconnect rate means no forward progress at all.
zslg_log_subscription_reorders_total	Counter	None	Subscription streams closed because a replica's delivered index went BACKWARDS (a reorder or duplicate). The replica log is gapless and single-writer and is read in index order, so this should always be zero: any non-zero value means local replica-log corruption on the serving replica, which warrants the wipe + new NodeId procedure.
zslg_log_writes_total	Counter	None	Entries appended/integrated, by log (replica = single-writer replica log, local = learner local store).
zslg_manifest_end_hlc_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of the stable watermark. Distance to hlc_now_physical_ms = stabilization lag.
zslg_manifest_entries_count	Gauge	Some(Count)	Snapshot files in the applied manifest. Steady growth without compaction = compaction wedged.
zslg_manifest_watermark_advanced_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix-epoch seconds of the last applied manifest-watermark advance (a snapshot committed + replicated), emitted by the raft state publisher on every node. THE snapshot alert per the design doc, evaluated as `time() - manifest_watermark_advanced_timestamp_seconds`: individual failure states are expected; that age growing is not.
zslg_membership_changes_total	Counter	None	Completed membership-change attempts by outcome.
zslg_membership_joint_exit_hlc_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of the armed joint-config drain bound (0 = not draining). A joint config may not collapse until manifest_end_hlc_physical_ms reaches this.
zslg_membership_retargets_total	Counter	None	Joint membership changes that recomputed their incoming set because a member of it stopped being live. Each retarget disarms the drain bound and discards the step-2 generation quorum, so a sustained rate means the change is restarting, not progressing.
zslg_node_last_seen_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix-epoch seconds of the last successful liveness probe reply from each peer (label node_id), per observing node. Cluster liveness is `time() - node_last_seen_timestamp_seconds`.
zslg_node_live	Gauge	None	1 while a peer (label node_id) is in the observing node's live set, 0 once probe failures drop it or it is evicted.
zslg_node_rejoins_total	Counter	None	Nodes with no local state that joined an existing cluster. Covers initial cluster formation, scale-out, and an operator wiping a node and rejoining it under a new NodeId after data loss — indistinguishable from inside the process. A LIFECYCLE counter, not an alarm: expect n-1 on a healthy n-node startup. Alert on unexpected CHANGES, not on a non-zero value.
zslg_object_store_request_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Object-store request latency by operation and outcome. Count per (op, state) doubles as the request counter.
zslg_object_store_transfer_bytes_total	Counter	Some(Bytes)	Bytes moved to/from the object store, by operation and by *why* the transfer happened. The `reason` split is the one that matters: reads run at a multiple of writes (26.7 GB GET against 12.3 GB PUT on one harness run), and `op` alone cannot say whether that is compaction re-reading what it just wrote or learners re-fetching a purged prefix — which have opposite fixes.
zslg_purged_entries_total	Counter	None	Entries removed by purge workers, by log type.
zslg_quorum_reads_total	Counter	None	Debounced quorum end_index reads by outcome.
zslg_raft_applied_log_index	Gauge	None	Last applied raft log index. Spread across nodes = apply lag.
zslg_raft_apply_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Raft apply_to_state_machine latency (membership writes + commit + fsync). The hottest I/O path after the replica log.
zslg_raft_build_snapshot_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Raft build_snapshot latency (read all meta + encode payload + commit + fsync). The most expensive single storage operation.
zslg_raft_client_write_timeouts_total	Counter	None	Fenced state writes abandoned because openraft's client_write did not resolve within raft_client_write_timeout. openraft has no deadline of its own, so this is the backstop against a partitioned leader stalling the integrator — and with it every leader job. Any non-zero rate warrants investigation.
zslg_raft_config_generation	Gauge	None	RaftGeneration of the applied state. Spread across nodes = state-replication lag.
zslg_raft_fenced_writes_total	Counter	None	Fenced state writes rejected by the apply-time generation CAS (StaleStateWrite). Expected around leadership churn; sustained rate without churn = bug.
zslg_raft_has_leader	Gauge	None	1 if this node currently observes an elected leader.
zslg_raft_in_joint_config	Gauge	None	1 while the applied membership is a joint config. Stuck at 1 = membership change wedged (doc: leader must resume from joint).
zslg_raft_install_snapshot_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Raft install_snapshot latency (decode + batch write + commit + fsync). Snapshot transfer cost on the receiving side.
zslg_raft_is_leader	Gauge	None	1 while this node is the raft leader. sum() != 1 for a sustained period means no (or a split) leader.
zslg_raft_is_member	Gauge	None	1 if this node is a voting member of the raft config, else 0. Cluster bootstrap alert: sum() staying 0.
zslg_raft_leader_changes_total	Counter	None	Leader changes observed by this node. Sustained rate = election churn.
zslg_raft_log_append_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Raft append_to_log latency (encode + batch + commit + fsync). Slow values delay replication to followers.
zslg_raft_log_read_empty_range_total	Counter	None	Raft-log reads for a non-empty index range that returned no entries, i.e. the whole range was already purged. openraft's DEFAULT limited_get_log_entries would unwrap that empty result and abort the process (our release profile sets panic=abort); we override it to return a StorageError instead, so openraft falls back to snapshot replication. Non-zero therefore means that fallback is being exercised — a follower fell behind the purge floor — not that anything crashed.
zslg_raft_term	Gauge	None	Current raft term as observed by this node.
zslg_read_entries_total	Counter	None	Entries streamed to readers, by the storage tier that served each (source=local_store|stable_log|object_store). Per-entry, unlike reads_total (per-request). object_store is non-zero only when a read reaches below the stable-log purge floor (design doc §794).
zslg_read_token_wait_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time read-your-writes tokens waited for the store to catch up.
zslg_read_token_wait_timeouts_total	Counter	None	Read-token waits that hit their timeout.
zslg_reads_total	Counter	None	Read requests served, by source (stable vs unstable).
zslg_replica_append_postcommit_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Post-fsync work per replica-log flush: frontier update, broadcast, oneshot ACKs, guard drop.
zslg_replica_append_queue_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time a write waited in the mpsc channel from append() enqueue to flush-loop dequeue (the batching wait). The non-storage part of replica_log_append_duration_seconds.
zslg_replica_log_append_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	ReplicaLogStore::append latency by outcome (enqueue to durable + broadcast). _count doubles as the append throughput.
zslg_replica_log_batch_build_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time to drain + encode one batch (BatchBuilder::build). The phase of the flush cycle no metric covered before.
zslg_replica_log_commit_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Segment-WAL append + fsync latency per replica-log flush batch (the engine's own durability fence). High values point at the disk, not at compaction.
zslg_replica_log_flush_bytes	Histogram	Some(Bytes)	Payload bytes per replica-log flush (one engine append batch). _sum is byte throughput, _count the number of flushes.
zslg_replica_log_flush_cycle_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Total time of one flush_one_batch_blocking body — batch build + append/fsync + fanout + completions. The flush thread's serial per-batch cost; the write-path serialization suspect.
zslg_replica_log_flush_entries	Histogram	Some(Count)	Entries batched into one replica-log flush. Distribution near 1 under load = batching starvation; near the cap = healthy batching.
zslg_replica_log_flush_trigger_total	Counter	None	Replica-log flushes by trigger reason (batch_full vs idle). A high idle share under load = the writer is starving the batch.
zslg_replica_log_last_flush_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix timestamp of the last replica-log flush. Grafana shows 'time since last flush' as time() minus this; a stall = stuck writer.
zslg_replica_log_queue_depth	Gauge	Some(Count)	Pending writes queued to the replica-log flush loop. Sustained growth = the flush loop cannot keep up (durability backpressure).
zslg_replica_nodes_count	Gauge	Some(Count)	Replica nodes in the applied config (both sets counted once under a joint config).
zslg_replica_outcome_dropped_total	Counter	None	Write outcomes the flush loop could not hand back because the connection's outcome channel was full. The write IS durable — the writer resends it on reconnect and the downstream dedups by data_id — so this costs a resend, never correctness. Non-zero means a connection's consumer stalled a full in-flight window deep, or its outcome channel is sized below the client's in-flight cap.
zslg_replica_write_requests_total	Counter	None	Replica-side write requests by outcome. fail_hlc_cutoff spikes are expected briefly after snapshot cutoffs. (Wall-clock skew is enforced at ingress as hlc_drift_too_large on the fresh per-RPC HLC, not here.)
zslg_replica_write_rpc_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Per-replica write-RPC latency + outcome (peer_id, outcome = success / rejected / failed). A failed bucket at ~unary_timeout = a black-hole/slow peer; a fast failed = refused/broken conn; rejected = replica-side reject. Attributes a write-rate dip to a specific replica.
zslg_rpc_accept_failed_total	Counter	None	Failed accept() calls on the RPC server, same `reason` vocabulary as rpc_connect_failed_total. The server-side half of a connectivity failure: peers reporting "refused"/"reset" while this counter climbs locates the fault on THIS node. "local_fd_exhausted" additionally means the accept loop is hot-spinning, since it retries immediately on error.
zslg_rpc_conn_checkout_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Fresh-dial cost paid on a unary keep-alive pool miss (DNS + TCP connect + socket setup). A pool hit reuses an idle connection and is not recorded here; see rpc_conn_reuse_total for the hit rate.
zslg_rpc_conn_reuse_total	Counter	None	Unary connection-pool checkouts by outcome. Label `state`: `hit` reused an idle connection, `miss` required a fresh dial. A low hit rate under load = connections churning (peer closing them, or the idle cap too small).
zslg_rpc_connect_failed_total	Counter	None	Failed outbound dials by reason. "refused" = the host answered but nothing is listening (peer down, or its accept loop is dead). "local_fd_exhausted" = EMFILE/ENFILE — OUR resource exhaustion, not a peer fault, and every dial fails until fds are released. "reset" = established then torn down, typically the peer's accept backlog overflowing. "timeout" = no answer within connect_timeout (wedged peer or blackholed route). "dns" = resolution failed, no socket was ever attempted. "unreachable" = no route. A sustained non-zero rate with a live peer means connections cannot be re-established at all — distinct from, and far more serious than, streams that end.
zslg_rpc_connections_active	Gauge	Some(Count)	Open TCP connections on the RPC server (unary keep-alive connections + persistent write/subscribe connections).
zslg_rpc_dns_resolve_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	DNS resolution time inside a dial. Called on every connect / reconnect; slow = resolver issue or DNS server unreachable.
zslg_rpc_open_stream_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Client open: dedicated connection dial + request send for a server-streaming RPC (subscribe); no response wait.
zslg_rpc_roundtrip_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Whole client-side unary RPC: open to first response frame.
zslg_rpc_server_request_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Server-side RPC handler latency by method.
zslg_rpc_server_requests_total	Counter	None	RPC requests handled, by method and outcome.
zslg_server_dispatch_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time a replicated write sat in the follower's server -> raft-append dispatch channel (frame received -> the raft submit picks it up). Directly timestamped on the queued object; grows under follower backpressure.
zslg_snapshot_compression_ratio	Gauge	None	Compression ratio of the last written snapshot (uncompressed_bytes / compressed_bytes). >1 means compression saved space; 1 means incompressible data.
zslg_snapshot_creation_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Snapshot creation latency per step (set_cutoff, await_quorum, write_object, commit) plus total.
zslg_snapshot_creation_total	Counter	None	Snapshot creation attempts by outcome (see SnapshotState). waiting_for_stable_log is backpressure, not failure: the leader's local stable log has not re-ingested its own last snapshot yet.
zslg_snapshot_entries_count	Gauge	Some(Count)	Entries in the last successful snapshot.
zslg_snapshot_size_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Size of the last successful snapshot object.
zslg_stable_log_append_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	StableLog::append latency by outcome. _count is the append throughput; _bucket the latency distribution.
zslg_stable_log_commit_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Segment-WAL append + fsync latency inside StableLog::append batches (the stable log's own durability fence).  High values = slow WAL fsyncs during snapshot load.
zslg_stable_log_end_hlc_advanced_timestamp_seconds	Gauge	Some(Seconds)	Unix-epoch seconds of the last stable-log upper-watermark (HLC end) advance on this node (new data going quorum-stable). Paired with the manifest stamp it separates 'idle' (both flat) from 'snapshot stalled' (this advancing, manifest flat).
zslg_stable_log_end_hlc_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of the stable log's end HLC (highest stabilized HLC). Distance to hlc_now_physical_ms = stabilization lag.
zslg_stable_log_purge_threshold_physical_ms	Gauge	Some(Milliseconds)	Physical-millisecond part of the HLC the stable log is being purged up to (manifest_watermark − purge_ttl, before the frontier clamp). Only moves when TTL purge is enabled; 0/absent means purge off or nothing aged out yet. Gap to stable_log_end_hlc_physical_ms = the live retention window still held locally (design doc §794).
zslg_stable_read_resyncs_total	Counter	None	Stable reads (`stable_tail` / `subscribe_stable_log`) that fell behind the append broadcast and re-entered historical replay from their own cursor. Benign by construction — the consumer sees no gap and no error, only latency — but a sustained rate means consumers are slower than the write rate or their subscriber queue (`stable_log_subscriber_queue_capacity`) is too shallow for the burst size, and each resync costs a re-scan of the durable log.
zslg_storage_actual_delayed_write_rate	Gauge	Some(Count)	rocksdb.actual-delayed-write-rate: the write-rate cap RocksDB is currently enforcing on this node's DB (bytes/sec), 0 when no throttle is active. Non-zero means the ENGINE is throttling writers — backpressure from the memtable/L0 limits, not the host. Cross-check against scrape duration to tell this apart from host starvation: the 2026-08-17 harness run could not, because nothing exported this.
zslg_storage_block_cache_usage_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	rocksdb.block-cache-usage: bytes currently held by the block cache, DB-wide (one cache per node, not per column family). A breakdown of storage_memory_charged_bytes, which also folds in memtables charged to the same cache.
zslg_storage_compaction_pending	Gauge	Some(Count)	rocksdb.compaction-pending: 1 if compaction is queued for this column family (cf), 0 otherwise. Sustained 1 alongside a growing storage_live_sst_files_size_bytes means compaction is falling behind.
zslg_storage_estimate_num_keys	Gauge	Some(Count)	rocksdb.estimate-num-keys: estimated live key count per column family (cf). An estimate (tombstones and duplicates are not resolved), not an exact count.
zslg_storage_live_sst_files_size_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	rocksdb.live-sst-files-size: on-disk size of the live SST files in this column family (cf). The disk-space counterpart to storage_memory_charged_bytes, which covers only the in-memory budget.
zslg_storage_memory_charged_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Bytes held by the storage engine's block cache — block, index and filter blocks plus the memtables charged to it. This is what the configured budget is judged against; read it beside process_memory_rss_bytes, whose excess over this is everything outside the budget (table readers, compaction buffers, iterator-pinned blocks, allocator fragmentation).
zslg_storage_memtable_flush_pending	Gauge	Some(Count)	rocksdb.mem-table-flush-pending: 1 if a memtable flush is queued for this column family (cf), 0 otherwise.
zslg_storage_num_immutable_memtable	Gauge	Some(Count)	rocksdb.num-immutable-mem-table: memtables that are full and waiting to be flushed, per column family (cf). A sustained climb means flush cannot keep up with the write rate.
zslg_storage_queue_capacity_bytes	Gauge	Some(Bytes)	Bytes this node's write-pipeline queues can hold at full depth: the replica write queue and one subscriber's live-tail ring at a 2 KiB reference record, plus the learner integrate channel and the residency of its DataId dedup ring. Deliberately NOT part of storage_memory_charged_bytes — that budget bounds RocksDB alone, and the two are separate sizing decisions (the engine stalls on its write-buffer manager, the pipeline blocks at submit). A planning figure, not a measurement: the queues are sized in slots, so bytes are depth times an assumed record size, and the per-subscriber ring term multiplies with the live subscriber count.
zslg_unstable_read_resyncs_total	Counter	None	Unstable-tail subscriptions (`subscribe_unstable_tail`) the local store's fan-out hung up on, which re-entered their cycle: re-read the watermark and rescan the whole unstable window. The consumer sees duplicates, never a gap. The under-pressure signal: a steadily rising rate means consumers cannot keep up with their queue depth and are paying an O(window) rescan each time.
zslg_write_duration_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	End-to-end client write latency: fan-out start to quorum resolution. Count per state doubles as the writes counter.
zslg_write_fanout_responses_total	Counter	None	Per-replica fan-out responses (ack / rejected / unreachable). Persistent unreachable share = degraded replica set.
zslg_write_stream_input_wait_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time a write sat in the write stream's input channel (coordinator fan pickup -> the blocking writer loop dequeues it). Directly timestamped on the queued object; ~0 when the writer loop keeps up.
zslg_write_stream_send_seconds	Histogram	Some(Seconds)	Time to feed + flush one batch of frames on the write-stream socket (leader -> follower). Grows when the follower's server isn't reading (TCP backpressure on the leader's send).
zslg_writer_fan_full_total	Counter	None	Feeder dropped an id because a replica's fan was full/gone (id resent on that replica's next reconnect prime). ~0 in steady state; sustained = a chronically-behind replica.
zslg_writer_inflight_permits	Gauge	Some(Count)	Held in-flight permits in the write coordinator. Pinned at the MAX_INFLIGHT cap = quorum can't keep up (backpressure).
zslg_writer_outcome_dropped_total	Counter	None	Outcome dropped because the per-connection outcome channel was full. ~0 in steady state; sustained = consumer (coordinator per-replica loop) is falling behind and writes will time out.
zslg_writer_replica_reopen_total	Counter	None	Per-replica write connections (re)opened. Steady-state growth = flapping connections.
zslg_writer_submit_dropped_total	Counter	None	Write dropped because the coordinator's submit channel was full. ~0 in steady state; sustained = the coordinator feeder is blocked and writes are backing up.
zslg_writes_in_flight	Gauge	Some(Count)	Writes between fan-out start and quorum resolution.
